<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="build-time-weaving" title="Build-time weaving">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>Needs the two dependencies from <a href="install.topic">Install</a>: the API and the
            annotation processor.</p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        Bind both weaving goals and Maven rewrites your compiled classes in place. The jar it
        packages is woven already. Your application starts with no agent and no extra flag.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="xml"><![CDATA[
<build>
    <plugins>
        <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
            <version>%compiler-plugin%</version>
            <configuration>
                <proc>full</proc>
            </configuration>
        </plugin>
        <plugin>
            <groupId>%group%</groupId>
            <artifactId>aether-weaver-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            <version>%version%</version>
            <executions>
                <execution>
                    <goals>
                        <goal>weave</goal>
                        <goal>weave-tests</goal>
                    </goals>
                </execution>
            </executions>
        </plugin>
    </plugins>
</build>
]]></code-block>

    <chapter title="Where the rewriting happens" id="lifecycle">
        <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["compile"] --> B["process-classes, where weave runs"]
    B --> C["target/classes rewritten in place"]
    C --> D["test-compile"]
    D --> E["process-test-classes, where weave-tests runs"]
    E --> F["target/test-classes rewritten"]
    F --> G["test, then package"]
    G --> H["Jar shipping woven bytecode"]
        </code-block>

        <p>
            Each goal applies the weaves its own directory records, and reads extension
            declarations off the whole classpath.
            <a href="weave-discovery.topic">How a weave is found</a> follows that record from
            <code>javac</code> to the goal.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <procedure title="Weave a build and check it" id="run">
        <step>
            <p>Put the block above in the pom of the module whose sources declare the weave.
                <a href="first-weave.topic">Your first weave</a> is three classes if you need
                one to try.</p>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Build.</p>
            <code-block lang="bash">
mvn verify
            </code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>The goal that found something to weave closes with a count.
                <a href="first-weave.topic">Your first weave</a> has no test sources, so only
                the main line prints.</p>
            <code-block lang="console">
[INFO] Aether Weaver: 1 weave, 1 class rewritten (main).
            </code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Audit the packaged jar. It prints what every woven class in it records
                about itself. That record is the proof the artefact carries the change.
                <a href="maven-goals.topic">Maven goals</a> has the goal.</p>
            <code-block lang="console"><![CDATA[
$ mvn %group%:aether-weaver-maven-plugin:%version%:audit \
      -Daether.weaver.artifact=target/app.jar
[INFO] fixture/Target.class
[INFO]   ← fixture.Greeting                INJECT   onGreet()V  →  greet()
[INFO]
[INFO] 1 class, 1 modification, fingerprint <64 hex characters>, no policy overrides
]]></code-block>
        </step>
    </procedure>

    <chapter title="No closing line at all" id="control">
        <p>
            No closing line at all means the goal found nothing to read, which is what
            happens when <code>javac</code> ran without the processor. Check
            <code>&lt;proc&gt;full&lt;/proc&gt;</code> and the
            <a href="install.topic">Install</a> dependencies.
            <a href="maven-goals.topic">Maven goals</a> covers the rest: stopping on an
            error, skipping a run, and seeing what each injection point matched.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Your test classes" id="tests">
        <p>
            <code>weave-tests</code> takes only the weaves declared in the test sources. Your
            main weaves stay on your main classes. The tests run against the woven ones.
            <a href="testing-woven-code.topic">Testing woven code</a> covers asserting on the
            result.
        </p>

        <warning>
            <p>
                Your main output is an entry on the test classpath like any other. So a
                weave declared in your main sources draws <code>AW3010</code> here. Nothing to
                do: no module can ask for itself by name.
                <a href="diagnostics-policy.topic">AW3xxx</a> has the reasoning. It stays
                quiet only where the test classes declare no weave and nothing on the test
                classpath declares an extension.
            </p>
        </warning>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="A target that lives in a dependency" id="dependencies">
        <p>
            <code>weaveDependencies</code> is off until you set it. The goal then touches no
            artefact somebody else published. Your target is planned and never reached, and all
            you get is a count.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="console">
[WARNING] Aether Weaver: 1 planned target were not found in main classes (1 of 2 woven)
        </code-block>

        <p>
            Switch it on for one run. Every resolved jar then goes through the same weaver.
            A signed one is refused as <code>AW3002</code>. Set
            <code>aether.weaver.allowSigned=true</code> too to weave it anyway, reported
            as <code>AW3020</code> instead.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="bash">
mvn verify -Daether.weaver.weaveDependencies=true
        </code-block>

        <p>
            Changed classes land under <code>target/aether-weaver/dependencies</code>, listed by
            <code>AW2501</code>. Add that directory ahead of the dependency jars wherever your build
            assembles a classpath. Otherwise nothing reads the woven copies, and the jars keep
            their published bytes either way. <a href="maven-goals.topic">Maven goals</a> has the
            parameters, signed artefacts included.
        </p>
    </chapter>

